  {
   "kind": "paragraph",
   "text": "The last time Anna Kovac took the sleeper from Vienna to Venice, she was nineteen and the carriages smelled of diesel and oranges. This spring she made the trip again, in a refitted cabin with a reading lamp, a folding desk and a door that locks. Nothing about the route had changed, except that people had once decided to give it up. Between 2010 and 2020 operators across the continent closed more than a dozen overnight routes. Cheap flights had made them look like relics, and replacing the worn rolling stock seemed impossible to justify. Then the arithmetic shifted. Fuel prices climbed, airports grew crowded, and a generation of travellers began to count the carbon in an itinerary as carefully as the euros. Austria's national railway was the first to bet on a revival. It bought used carriages from a bankrupt competitor, rebuilt them, and filled them within a season. Sweden, France and Italy followed, each wary of repeating the mistakes of the past. The cabins must be quiet, the timetable must make sense, and the price must not feel like a punishment. A sleeper berth costs several times what a seat does to run. A carriage that carries sixty people by day carries only twenty-four at night, and it spends the small hours standing in sidings while crews rest. Operators say a typical overnight train needs a public subsidy of about forty euros for each passenger it carries, roughly the price of the ticket itself. Supporters answer that a flight does not pay for the damage it does either. They point to a study of the Vienna to Brussels corridor, where a night train would emit one fifth of the carbon of the same journey by air, and where more than nine in ten travellers said they would choose it if the fare were within a quarter of the cheapest flight. On the night of her trip Kovac left Vienna at 21:40 and was asleep before Graz. The attendant, a retired schoolteacher named Pavel Horak, had made up her bunk with a wool blanket and left a bottle of water and a card with the breakfast hours. He said the youngest passengers were the most grateful, because they had never travelled any other way than by plane. The next morning the train ran along the edge of the lagoon, which was grey and still, and arrived in Venice eleven minutes early. Kovac, who had slept better than she had for months, ate a roll in the carriage and watched the water slide by. She said she would take the sleeper again in the autumn, and that she had already booked a berth for her mother. Operators are learning that the product is as much hotel as railway. The new cabins have blackout blinds, sound-proofed doors and small washbasins, and the most expensive carry a shower. Booking systems now sell",
