  {
   "kind": "paragraph",
   "text": "Long-distance travel changed quickly once the state-owned railways discovered that half-empty carriages cost almost as much to run as crowded ones. Managers who had specialised in high-speed services were suddenly responsible for overnight operations, and many of them considered the assignment a punishment. Nevertheless, passenger numbers improved, particularly on international connections where short-haul flights had become unreliable. Twenty-four refurbished carriages entered service in the autumn, each with sound-proofed compartments and individually controlled ventilation. Independent researchers subsequently interviewed approximately three hundred travellers about their experiences. Dissatisfaction concentrated on reservations: the well-known booking system frequently malfunctioned, and replacement confirmations occasionally disappeared altogether. Environmental organisations nonetheless recommended the trains enthusiastically, emphasising extraordinary reductions in emissions compared with conventional alternatives.",
